      "Abstract": {
        "CopyrightInformation": "© RSNA, 2011",
        "AbstractText": [
          {
            "NlmCategory": "OBJECTIVE",
            "Label": "PURPOSE",
            "content": "To evaluate the feasibility and efficacy of computed tomography (CT)-guided radiofrequency (RF) neurolysis (RFN) in the management of refractory inguinal neuralgia."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
            "Label": "MATERIALS AND METHODS",
            "content": "Local institutional review board approval was obtained and written informed consent was waived. Twelve patients 26-75 years of age with chronic (>6 months) inguinal pain refractory to specific medication were included between 2005 and 2011. Data on patient demographics, clinical history, and pain management history were retrospectively assessed. Pain was measured on a visual analog scale (VAS) from 0 to 10 before and immediately after the procedure and at 1, 3, 6, 9 and 12 months. Diagnosis was always confirmed by a positive nerve block test result. Ambulatory CT-guided RF was the rule."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "RESULTS",
            "Label": "RESULTS",
            "content": "Sixteen RFN procedures were performed. Pain was present for an average of 3.2 years (range: 2-8 years) prior to initial RFN. Mean VAS score before the procedure was 7.75 of 10. Immediate pain relief of 100% was achieved in all patients. Pain reduction at 1-, 3-, 6-, 9-, and 12-month follow-up was statistically significant. Important pain reduction (≥80%) was obtained in 75% of RFN procedures at 6-month follow-up and in 50% of cases at 12 months. The mean duration of pain relief was 11.8 months after RFN, with a maximum average pain reduction of 84.5%. No complications were noted during or after the procedure."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
            "Label": "CONCLUSION",
            "content": "RFN with CT guidance is an effective technique in the management of refractory inguinal pain with lasting satisfactory pain reduction; it may be considered as an alternative treatment to surgery. These results should be confirmed in a controlled trial with a larger series of patients."
          }
        ]
      },
